        'synopsis' => 'A king hires Sherlock Holmes to steal back a photograph from the one woman who ever outsmarted him.',
        'text' => "To Sherlock Holmes she is always THE woman. In his eyes she eclipses and predominates the whole of her sex. It was not that he loved her. All emotions, and that one particularly, were abhorrent to his cold, precise mind. He was the most perfect reasoning and observing machine the world has seen, and a strong emotion in a nature such as his would be like grit in a sensitive instrument. And yet there was but one woman to him, and that woman was Irene Adler.\n\nI had seen little of Holmes since my marriage. So one March evening, passing our old lodgings in Baker Street and seeing his tall figure pacing behind the lit window, I rang the bell and climbed the seventeen steps I had walked a hundred times without counting.\n\nHis manner was not warm, but he was glad to see me. He looked me over in his singular introspective fashion.\n\n\"Wedlock suits you,\" he remarked. \"I think, Watson, that you have put on seven and a half pounds since I saw you.\"\n\n\"Seven!\" I answered.\n\n\"And in practice again, I observe. How do you suppose I know?\"\n\n\"How DO you know?\"\n\n\"I see it, I deduce it. On the inside of your left shoe the leather is scored by six almost parallel cuts, made by someone who scraped crusted mud off your soles carelessly. You have been out in vile weather, and you have a careless servant girl.\"\n\n\"When I hear you give your reasons,\" I laughed, \"the thing seems so ridiculously simple that I could easily do it myself. And yet I believe my eyes are as good as yours.\"\n\n\"Quite so. You see, but you do not observe. How often have you seen the steps which lead up to this room?\"\n\n\"Well, some hundreds of times.\"\n\n\"Then how many are there?\"\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\n\"Quite so! You have not observed. But I KNOW there are seventeen steps, because I have both seen and observed.\"\n\nHe handed me a sheet of thick pink notepaper. \"It came by the last post,\" he said. \"Read it aloud.\"\n\n\"There will call upon you tonight,\" I read