f a guinea if you do it in twenty minutes!\" Minutes later Miss Adler flew past him in her own carriage crying the same address. Holmes had followed, slipped into the church, and been dragged to the altar as witness.\n\n\"They wanted a witness or the wedding would not be legal,\" he chuckled. \"So I assisted in the secure tying up of Irene Adler to Godfrey Norton. The bride gave me a sovereign, and I mean to wear it on my watch chain in memory of the occasion.\"\n\nThat evening Holmes appeared at Briony Lodge dressed as a kind old clergyman. What followed was theater, and every person loitering in Serpentine Avenue was an actor paid by Holmes. As Irene Adler stepped from her carriage, two men fought over the right to open her door. Holmes dashed forward to shield her, fell with red paint streaming across his face, and lay bleeding in the street.\n\n\"Is the poor gentleman much hurt?\" she cried.\n\n\"He can't lie in the street. May we bring him in, marm?\"\n\n\"Surely. Bring him into the sitting room. This way, please!\"\n\nThey bore him to a sofa by the window. I stood outside at the spot he had chosen for me, ashamed of deceiving someone so gracious, my hand around a plumber's smoke rocket. Through the window I watched him raise his hand, and at the signal I hurled the rocket into the room and shouted the word he had drilled into me.\n\n\"Fire!\"\n\nThe whole street took up the cry. And through the smoke I glimpsed the lady herself darting not to the door, but to a recess beside the bell pull, tearing back a sliding panel, reaching for the thing she valued most.\n\n\"False alarm,\" Holmes called smoothly, and slipped away to join me at the corner.\n\n\"The photograph is in a recess behind a sliding panel, just above the right bell pull,\" he told me, walking fast. \"When a woman thinks her house is on fire, her instinct is to rush to the thing she values most. She showed me, as I knew she would. Tomorrow morning I shall call with the King and hand him his property