        'synopsis' => 'A pawnbroker is paid four pounds a week to copy an encyclopedia, and only Sherlock Holmes sees the crime hidden inside the joke.',
        'text' => "I had called upon my friend Mr. Sherlock Holmes one day in autumn and found him in deep conversation with a very stout, elderly gentleman with fiery red hair. Holmes pulled me into the room before I could retreat.\n\n\"You could not possibly have come at a better time, my dear Watson,\" he said cordially. \"Mr. Jabez Wilson has been good enough to call upon me this morning and begin a narrative which promises to be one of the most singular I have listened to for some time.\"\n\nI studied the visitor while he fumbled a dirty newspaper from his pocket. An ordinary tradesman in every way except one. His hair blazed like a bonfire.\n\n\"Beyond the obvious facts that he has done manual labor, that he takes snuff, that he is a Freemason, that he has been in China, and that he has done a great deal of writing lately, I can deduce nothing else,\" said Holmes.\n\n\"How, in the name of good fortune, did you know all that?\" cried Wilson.\n\n\"Your right hand is larger than your left, sir. The arc-and-compass breastpin betrays the order. A Chinese coin hangs from your watch chain, and the fish tattooed above your wrist was stained pink in a way only Chinese artists use.\"\n\n\"Well, I never! I thought you had done something clever, but I see it was all observation.\" He flattened the paper on his knee and planted a thick finger halfway down the column. \"Here it is. This is what began it all.\"\n\nTO THE RED-HEADED LEAGUE. On account of the bequest of the late Ezekiah Hopkins of Lebanon, Pennsylvania, there is now another vacancy open which entitles a member of the League to a salary of four pounds a week for purely nominal services. All red-headed men who are sound in body and mind and above the age of twenty-one are eligible. Apply in person on Monday at eleven to Duncan Ross, at the offices of the League, seven Pope's Court, Fleet Street.\n\n\"What on earth does this mean?\" I gasped after reading it twice.\n\nHolmes chuckled and wriggled in his chair, his habit when in high spirits. \"It is a little off the beaten track, isn't it? Now, Mr. Wilson, tell us the effect this advertisement had upon your fortunes.\"\n\nWilson kept a small pawnbroker's shop in Saxe-Coburg Square and one assistant, Vincent Spaulding, a quick fellow willing to work for half wages just to learn the business. Eight weeks earlier Spaulding had come down to the office waving that very paper.\n\n\"'I wish to the Lord, Mr. Wilson, that I was a red-headed man,' says he. 'Here's a vacancy worth a little fortune to any man who gets it.'\"\n\nThe League had been founded, so the story went, by a strange American millionaire who was himself red-headed and left his enormous fortune to provide easy berths for men whose hair matched his own. Wilson had walked to Fleet Street with Spaulding and found the street choked with men of every shade, straw and lemon and orange and liver and clay. But few carried the true vivid flame-colored tint of Jabez Wilson.\n\nIn a bare office sat a small man whose head was even redder than his own, a Mr. Duncan Ross. Ross seized Wilson's hair in both hands and tugged until he yelled.\n\n\"There is water in your eyes,\" said Ross. \"All is as it should be. But we have to be careful. We have twice been deceived by wigs and once by paint.\"\n\nThe duties were simple. He must arrive at ten and stay until two, and never once leave the office during those hours, or he would forfeit the position forever.\n\n\"And the work?\" asked Wilson.\n\n\"Is to copy out the Encyclopedia Britannica. Will you be ready tomorrow?\"\n\n\"Certainly,\" said Wilson.\n\nEvery morning for eight weeks he copied, starting at the letter A. Abbots, Archery, Armor, Architecture and Attica filled shelf after shelf of foolscap. Each Saturday Ross paid him four golden sovereigns. Then one morning he arrived and found the door locked, with a square of cardboard tacked to the panel.\n\nHe held it up. THE RED-HEADED LEAGUE IS DISSOLVED. October 9, 1890.\n\nHolmes and I roared with laughter until our client flushed to the roots of his flaming head.\n\n\"If you can do nothing better than laugh at me, I can go elsewhere!\"\n\n\"No, no. I really wouldn't miss your case for the world,\" Holmes protested. \"Tell me, what did the landlord know of Mr. Duncan Ross?\"\n\nNothing. The man had called himself William Morris, used the room temporarily, and vanished. The address he left behind turned out to be a factory of artificial knee caps.\n\n\"And this assistant of yours,\" said Holmes, sitting forward with sudden excitement. \"Describe him again. Small, quick, no hair on his face?\"\n\n\"Yes, sir. And a white splash of acid upon his forehead.\"\n\n\"I thought as much. Have you ever observed that his ears are pierced for earrings?\"\n\n\"A traveler did that when he was a lad, he told me.\"\n\n\"Hum.\" Holmes sank back into deep thought. Then he sprang up. \"This is quite a three pipe problem, and I beg that you won't speak to me for fifty minutes.\" He curled up with his black clay pipe thrusting out like the bill of some strange bird. At the end of the fifty minutes he opened his eyes.\n\n\"Sarasate plays at St. James's Hall this afternoon,\" he remarked. \"Put on your hat and come. German music is on the program, and it is introspective. I want to introspect.\"\n\nBut first we rode out to Saxe-Coburg Square, a poky little place of dingy brick houses where three gilt balls announced JABEZ WILSON above the door. Holmes stood staring at the house, then knocked. The bright-faced assistant himself opened it.\n\n\"Thank you,\" said Holmes. \"I only wished to ask how you would go from here to the Strand.\"\n\n\"Third right, fourth left,\" answered Spaulding promptly, closing the door.\n\n\"Smart fellow, that,\" observed Holmes as we walked away. \"In my judgment the fourth smartest man in London, and for daring I am not sure he has not a claim to be third.\"\n\n\"Then it WAS his face you wanted to see.\"\n\n\"Not his face. His knees were what I wished to see.\" We had turned the corner into a roaring main street of fine shops. \"My dear doctor, this is a time for observation, not for talk. We are spies in an enemy's country. We know something of Saxe-Coburg Square. Let us now explore the part which lies behind it.\"\n\nBehind the shabby square lay one of the great arteries of the city, and among the shops Holmes counted them aloud. A tobacconist, a newspaper shop, a branch of the City and Suburban Bank.\n\nThat evening I found two strangers waiting at Baker Street, Peter Jones of Scotland Yard and a solemn bank director named Merryweather, who complained mournfully that this was the first Saturday night in twenty-seven years he had missed his whist.\n\n\"I think you will find,\" said Holmes, \"that you will play for a higher stake tonight than you have ever done yet. For you, Mr. Merryweather, the stake will be thirty thousand pounds. For you, Jones, it will be John Clay, the murderer, thief, smasher and forger.\"\n\nWe followed the director down winding stone steps into the great cellar vault of the bank, piled with crates of French gold coin borrowed from the Bank of France and never unpacked. Holmes knelt and examined the cracks between the stone flags with his lens, then dimmed his lantern.\n\n\"We have at least an hour before us. They can hardly move until the good pawnbroker is safely in bed. Sit on that crate, doctor, put your revolver ready, and whatever happens, do not interfere.\"\n\nHe shot the slide across the lantern and left us in absolute darkness. Never have I known such a wait. Then my eyes caught a lurid spark between the stones. The spark lengthened to a yellow line, a gash opened without any sound, and a pale hand appeared, feeling about in the circle of light. The hand withdrew. There came a rending, tearing sound, one broad white stone turned over, and a clean-shaven face looked out while a second man hoisted himself through the hole.\n\n\"It's all clear,\" he whispered. \"Have you the chisel and the bags? Great Scott! Jump, Archie, jump, and I'll swing for it!\"\n\nHolmes sprang and seized him by the collar. The other dove back down the hole, and Jones clutched at his coat tails. A revolver flashed out, but Holmes's hunting crop came down on the man's wrist and the pistol clinked harmlessly on the stone.\n\n\"It's no use, John Clay,\" said Holmes blandly. \"You have no chance at all.\"\n\n\"So I see,\" answered the criminal with perfect coolness. \"I fancy my pal is all right, though I see you have got his coat tails.\"\n\n\"Three men are waiting for him at the door.\"\n\n\"Oh, indeed! You seem to have done the thing very completely. I must compliment you.\"\n\n\"And I you,\" said Holmes. \"Your red-headed idea was very new and effective.\"\n\n\"I beg that you will not touch me with your filthy hands,\" said our prisoner as the handcuffs clicked. \"You may not be aware that I have royal blood in my veins. Have the goodness, also, when you address me, always to say 'sir' and 'please.'\"\n\n\"All right,\" said Jones, staring. \"Would you PLEASE, sir, march upstairs, where we can get a cab to carry your Highness to the police station?\"\n\n\"That is better,\" said John Clay serenely. He swept us a bow and marched off between his captors.\n\nLater, over whisky and soda in Baker Street, Holmes laid the chain out link by link. The fantastic League could only have one purpose, to keep a dull pawnbroker out of his shop four hours every day. The assistant had come for half wages because he wanted that cellar, where he spent months digging a tunnel from Saxe-Coburg Square to the bank behind it. The photograph in the window told nothing, but the knees of the man's trousers spoke of hours of burrowing, and the worn knees belonged to the fourth smartest man in London. When the League dissolved itself, Holmes knew the tunnel was finished and Saturday night chosen because it gave thieves two days of escape.\n\n\"But how could you tell they would strike tonight?\" I asked.\n\n\"They closed their offices the moment the tunnel was done. They had to use it at once, before the gold could be moved. Everything pointed to tonight.\"\n\n\"You reasoned it out beautifully,\" I cried. \"It is such a long chain, and yet every link rings true.\"\n\n\"It saved me from boredom,\" said Holmes, yawning. \"My life is spent in one long effort to escape from the commonplaces of existence, and these little problems help me to do so. As Gustave Flaubert wrote to George Sand, the man is nothing, the work is everything.\"",
