        'synopsis' => 'A stolen jewel turns up inside a Christmas goose, and Sherlock Holmes must choose between justice and mercy.',
        'text' => "I called upon my friend Sherlock Holmes on the second morning after Christmas and found him lounging on his sofa in a purple dressing gown, studying a very seedy and disreputable hard felt hat that hung on the back of a chair, cracked in several places. A lens and forceps lay beside it.\n\n\"You are engaged,\" said I. \"Perhaps I interrupt you.\"\n\n\"Not at all. I am glad to have a friend with whom I can discuss my results.\" He jerked his thumb toward the old hat. \"There are points in connection with it which are not entirely without interest and even instruction.\"\n\n\"No crime, I hope.\"\n\n\"No, no. No crime. Only one of those whimsical little incidents which will happen when you have four million human beings all jostling each other within the space of a few square miles.\"\n\nThe hat had come to him through Peterson, the commissionaire from our hotel. On Christmas morning Peterson saw a tall man staggering home through the gaslight with a white goose slung over his shoulder. A gang of roughs knocked off his hat, the man swung his stick wildly and smashed a shop window, and in the panic he dropped his bird and ran. Peterson was left holding the field of battle, and the spoils of victory.\n\n\"The spoils being this battered hat and a most unimpeachable Christmas goose,\" said Holmes.\n\n\"But surely he restored them to their owner?\"\n\n\"There lies the problem. A card tied to the bird's leg said FOR MRS. HENRY BAKER, and the initials H. B. are scrawled inside the hat. But there are thousands of Bakers in this city. So the goose went to Peterson's oven, and I keep the hat of the unknown gentleman who lost his Christmas dinner.\"\n\nHe tossed the hat across to me. \"You know my methods. What can you gather about the man who wore this?\"\n\n\"I can see nothing.\"\n\n\"On the contrary, Watson, you can see everything. You fail, however, to reason from what you see. That the man was highly intellectual is obvious. That he was once fairly well to do but has fallen upon evil days is also plain. He had foresight once, and less now. His wife has ceased to love him. He leads a sedentary life, is middle aged, has grizzled hair cut within the last few days, anoints it with lime cream, and there is no gas laid on at his house.\"\n\n\"You are certainly joking, Holmes!\"\n\n\"Not in the least.\" He clapped the hat upon his own head. It slid down to the bridge of his nose. \"It is a question of cubic capacity. A man with so large a brain must have something in it. The hat is three years old and of the very best quality. If he could afford such a hat then, and has bought none since, he has assuredly gone down in the world. As for the wife, when your hat goes unbrushed for weeks and your wife lets you out of the house in such a state, I shall fear that you too have lost her affection. And the tallow stains, five of them, show a man who climbs dark stairs with a guttering candle, not gas jets.\"\n\n\"It is very ingenious,\" I laughed. \"But since no crime has been committed and no harm done save the loss of a goose, all this seems rather a waste of energy.\"\n\nHolmes opened his mouth to reply when the door burst open and Peterson rushed in, dazed with astonishment.\n\n\"The goose, Mr. Holmes! See here, sir! See what my wife found in its crop!\"\n\nOn the center of his palm lay a brilliant blue stone, smaller than a bean but shining like an electric point in the dark hollow of his hand.\n\nSherlock Holmes sat up with a whistle. \"By Jove, Peterson! This is treasure trove indeed. It is more than a precious stone. It is THE precious stone.\"\n\n\"Not the Countess of Morcar's blue carbuncle!\" I cried.\n\n\"Precisely so. And five days ago it vanished from her room at the Hotel Cosmopolitan.\" He smoothed out one of his newspapers and read us the report. A plumber named John Horner had been summoned to repair the grate in the countess's room. When he was briefly left alone, the jewel case stood forced and empty. The hotel attendant James Ryder raised the alarm, Horner was arrested, and the stone could not be found. The case had been sent up for trial.\n\n\"So much for the police court,\" said Holmes thoughtfully. \"Our question runs from a rifled jewel case at one end to the crop of a goose in Tottenham Court Road at the other. We must find Mr. Henry Baker.\"\n\nAn advertisement brought Baker himself to Baker Street that evening, a large man with rounded shoulders and a broad intelligent face, his coat buttoned to the chin against the cold. He took the replacement goose Holmes offered with relief and refused the feathers and crop of his own bird as useless. But when Holmes asked where so fine a goose had come from, the answer set us on the trail. A goose club at the Alpha Inn near the Museum. The landlord Windigate. And Windigate bought his birds from a salesman named Breckinridge in Covent Garden.\n\n\"Remember, Watson,\" said Holmes as we buttoned our coats against the bitter night, \"though we have so homely a thing as a goose at one end of this chain, we have at the other a man who may get seven years' penal servitude unless we can establish his innocence.\"\n\nCovent Garden was nearly shut for the night. Breckinridge, a sharp-faced man with trim whiskers, was putting up his shutters, and the mere word geese made him furious.\n\n\"'Where are the geese?' and 'Who did you sell the geese to?'\" he snarled. \"One would think they were the only geese in the world!\"\n\nHolmes changed his weapon. He offered to bet five pounds that the goose he had eaten was country bred. The salesman snapped that every bird he sent the Alpha was town bred, and to prove it fetched his books and read out his supplier. Mrs. Oakshott, 117 Brixton Road, poultry supplier. Twenty-four geese sold December 22nd to Mr. Windigate of the Alpha.\n\nHolmes threw down the sovereign he had lost, walked away, and laughed his silent hearty laugh under a lamp post. \"If I had put a hundred pounds before that man he would never have told me so much as he did for the sake of winning a wager. But look there!\"\n\nA little rat-faced fellow was standing in the lamplight of the stall, cringing under Breckinridge's fury.\n\n\"I've had enough of you and your geese!\" the salesman bellowed. \"Ask Mrs. Oakshott, or ask the King of Proosia for all I care. Get out of this!\"\n\nHolmes touched the little man on the shoulder in the shadows beyond the lamps.\n\n\"My name is Sherlock Holmes. It is my business to know what other people don't know. You are tracing some geese sold by Mrs. Oakshott of Brixton Road to Breckinridge, by him to Windigate of the Alpha, and by him to his club.\"\n\n\"Oh sir, you are the very man whom I have longed to meet,\" cried the fellow with quivering fingers.\n\n\"And your name?\"\n\n\"John Robinson,\" he answered with a sidelong glance.\n\n\"No, no. The real name,\" said Holmes sweetly. \"It is always awkward doing business with an alias.\"\n\nThe white cheeks flushed. \"My real name is James Ryder.\"\n\nThe head attendant of the Hotel Cosmopolitan sat trembling in the cab all the way back to Baker Street. There, beside the roaring fire, Holmes drew the blue carbuncle from his strong box, where it shone like a cold blue star.\n\n\"The game is up, Ryder,\" he said quietly. \"You and the maid Cusack arranged a small job for the plumber Horner, rifled the jewel case yourselves, and let suspicion fall on a man who already had one old conviction. Then you carried the stone away in your waistcoat pocket. What became of it next?\"\n\nRyder threw himself on the rug and clutched at Holmes's knees. \"For God's sake, have mercy! Think of my father! Think of my mother! It would break their hearts. Oh, don't bring it into court!\"\n\n\"Get back into your chair,\" said Holmes sternly. \"You thought little enough of poor Horner in the dock. But tell me the truth. How came the stone into the goose?\"\n\nWhite and shaking, Ryder told it. Afraid to keep the gem on him, he had run to his sister Mrs. Oakshott's yard in Brixton, where she fattened poultry for market. She had promised him the pick of her geese for Christmas. Behind the shed he seized a great white bird with a barred tail, forced its bill open, and pushed the stone down its throat. The bird struggled loose and vanished among the flock, and when his sister called him in he pretended nothing.\n\n\"'We've set yours aside for you,' she said. 'Jem's bird, we call it. The big white one over yonder.'\"\n\n\"Then I'll take THAT one, the one I was handling,\" Ryder had insisted. And so he had killed and carried off the wrong goose entirely, for there were two barred-tailed birds in the yard and no soul alive could tell them apart. At his friend's rooms they cut the goose open and found nothing. My heart turned to water, he said, and he had run half mad through London ever since, hunting a bird worth more than all the rest of the flock together.\n\nHis face sank into his hands, and his sobs shook the little room. There was a long silence, broken only by the tapping of Holmes's fingertips on the table edge.\n\nThen my friend rose, threw open the door, and pointed into the darkness. \"Get out!\"\n\n\"What, sir! Oh, Heaven bless you!\"\n\n\"No more words. Get out!\"\n\nThere was a rush, a clatter on the stairs, and the rattle of running footfalls dying up the street.\n\n\"After all, Watson, I am not retained by the police to supply their deficiencies,\" said Holmes, reaching for his pipe. \"This fellow will not dare appear against Horner, so the charge must collapse. I suppose that I am commuting a felony, but it is just possible that I am saving a soul. Send him to jail now and you make him a jail bird for life. Besides, it is the season of forgiveness. Chance has given us a most singular and whimsical problem, and its solution is its own reward.\"",
