        'synopsis' => 'A terrified young woman begs Sherlock Holmes to explain why her sister died whispering of a speckled band, and why the same whistle now sounds outside her own door.',
        'text' => "Of all the strange cases in my notes about my friend Sherlock Holmes, I can recall none more singular than that of the Roylotts of Stoke Moran. It happened early in our friendship, when we were still bachelors sharing rooms in Baker Street, and I kept it secret for years out of a promise made to a brave lady.\n\nI woke one April morning in 1883 to find Holmes standing fully dressed beside my bed.\n\n\"Very sorry to wake you, Watson,\" said he, \"but a client has arrived in a considerable state of excitement, and I thought you would wish to follow this case from the outset.\"\n\nA lady dressed in black and heavily veiled rose from the window seat as we entered.\n\n\"Good morning, madam. My name is Sherlock Holmes. Pray draw up to the fire and let me order you some hot coffee, for I observe that you are shivering.\"\n\n\"It is not cold which makes me shiver,\" said the woman in a low voice. \"It is fear, Mr. Holmes. It is terror.\"\n\nShe raised her veil. Her face was drawn and gray, her restless eyes those of some hunted animal.\n\n\"You must not fear. We shall soon set matters right. You have come in by train this morning, I see. You started early, after a good drive along heavy roads.\"\n\n\"Do you know me, then?\"\n\n\"I observe the second half of a return ticket in the palm of your left glove. And the left sleeve of your jacket is spattered with fresh mud in seven places. Only a dog cart throws up mud in that way.\"\n\n\"You are perfectly correct. Oh sir, I can stand this strain no longer. I shall go mad if it continues.\" Her story poured out then, and every word of it is seared in my memory.\n\nHer name was Helen Stoner. She lived with her stepfather, Dr. Grimesby Roylott, last survivor of an ancient Surrey family that had wasted itself away until nothing remained but a few acres and a crumbling manor house. The doctor had been a successful physician in Calcutta once, until in a fit of rage he killed a servant and served a long term in prison. He had come home a changed man, morose and violent, the terror of his village. He kept wild beasts from India roaming his grounds, a cheetah and a baboon, and he let wandering travelers camp among the brambles because they amused him. Once he hurled the village blacksmith over a parapet into a stream, and only money paid in silence kept it out of the courts.\n\nHelen's mother had been a widow with twin daughters and a good income. She left everything to Dr. Roylott while the girls lived under his roof, with one provision. On the day either girl married, she would be entitled to an annual sum of her own. Eight years ago the mother died in a railway accident, and the doctor took her daughters to live in the ruinous old house.\n\n\"My sister Julia is dead,\" said Miss Stoner simply. \"She died two years ago, and it is of her death I wish to speak.\"\n\nJulia had become engaged to a major of marines, and the wedding was a fortnight away when the fatal night came. The bedrooms of the inhabited wing all opened onto one corridor. That night their stepfather retired early, smoking his strong Indian cigars, and Julia sat talking happily in Helen's room until eleven o'clock. At the door she paused and looked back.\n\n\"'Tell me, Helen,' said she, 'have you ever heard anyone whistle in the dead of the night?'\n\n\"Never, said I.\n\n\"'Because during the last few nights I have always, about three in the morning, heard a low clear whistle. Perhaps from the next room. Perhaps from the lawn.'\n\n\"'It must be those wretched campers in the plantation.'\n\n\"She smiled, closed my door, and a few moments later I heard her key turn in the lock.\"\n\nBoth sisters always locked themselves in at night. With a baboon loose on the grounds, who would not?\n\nThat night a storm broke over the house. Helen lay listening to the howling wind when suddenly, amid the hubbub of the gale, there burst forth the wild scream of a terrified woman. She knew her sister's voice. As she flung open her own door she seemed to hear the low whistle, and then a clanging sound, as if a mass of metal had fallen. Down the passage her sister's door stood open, and by the light of the corridor lamp Helen saw her appear in the doorway, face blanched with terror, hands groping for help, swaying like a drunkard.\n\n\"She fell at my feet writhing in terrible pain, and suddenly she shrieked out in a voice which I shall never forget. OH, MY GOD! HELEN! IT WAS THE BAND! THE SPECKLED BAND! There was something else she would have said, and she stabbed her finger toward my stepfather's room, but a fresh spasm choked the words. Before the doctor from the village could arrive she was gone.\"\n\nThe door had been bolted inside. The shutters were secured with iron bars, the walls were solid, the floor solid, the chimney too narrow. No marks of violence, no trace of poison. The coroner could find no cause of death at all, and wrote down what Helen believed herself, that her sister had died of pure fear and nervous shock.\n\n\"And two days ago,\" said Miss Stoner, rising in agitation, \"repairs began in the west wing, and I was moved into my sister's room. Into HER very bed. Last night, lying awake in the dark, I heard the low whistle which was the herald of her death. I have come to you because I am afraid to sleep another night under that roof.\"\n\nHolmes gently pushed back the lace frill at her wrist. Five little livid spots were printed on the white skin, the marks of four fingers and a thumb.\n\n\"You have been cruelly used,\" said he.\n\n\"He is a hard man, and perhaps he hardly knows his own strength,\" she murmured, covering the marks.\n\n\"Dark enough and sinister enough,\" Holmes said when she had gone. Then the door crashed open, and a huge man filled the doorway, a black top hat brushing the lintel, a hunting crop swinging in his fist. His great yellow face seared with wrinkles turned slowly from me to Holmes.\n\n\"Which of you is Holmes?\"\n\n\"My name, sir, but you have the advantage of me.\"\n\n\"I am Dr. Grimesby Roylott, of Stoke Moran. My stepdaughter has been here. What has she been saying to you?\"\n\n\"It is a little cold for the time of year,\" said Holmes pleasantly.\n\n\"What has she been saying?\" screamed the old man.\n\n\"The crocuses promise well, I have heard.\"\n\n\"Holmes, the meddler! Holmes, the busybody! Holmes, the Scotland Yard Jack-in-office!\"\n\n\"Your conversation is most entertaining,\" said my friend. \"When you go out, close the door, for there is a decided draft.\"\n\n\"Don't you dare to meddle with my affairs. I am a dangerous man to fall foul of. See here!\" He snatched the poker from the fireplace and bent it into a curve with his bare hands, hurled it down, and strode out.\n\n\"He seems a very amiable person,\" laughed Holmes, straightening the poker again with one effort. \"That visit gives zest to our investigation.\"\n\nHis own visit that morning had been to Doctors' Commons, where he read the will of Helen's mother and worked out the sums against falling prices. The estate that had once paid eleven hundred pounds now yielded barely seven hundred and fifty. Each daughter's marriage clause cost him two hundred and fifty a year. One marriage would cripple him. Two would make him a pauper.\n\n\"He has the very strongest motive for standing in the way of anything of the sort,\" said Holmes. \"Bring your revolver, Watson. An Eley's Number Two is an excellent argument with gentlemen who can twist steel pokers into knots.\"\n\nWe found Miss Stoner waiting on the path near Stoke Moran, a gray lichen-blotched pile with wings like the claws of a crab, one wing already boarded and caved in. In her bedroom Holmes went down on his knees and crawled about with his lens, examining the boards, the paneling, the bed. He tugged at the thick bell rope that hung beside the pillow.\n\n\"Why, it is a dummy,\" said he. \"Not even attached to a wire. And see, it is fastened to a hook just above the little opening of the ventilator.\"\n\n\"How very absurd. I never noticed that before.\"\n\n\"A ventilator which does not ventilate. It opens not upon the air outside, but into the next room.\" His eyes grew very grave.\n\nIn Dr. Roylott's chamber beyond the wall they found a wooden chair scarred as if a man habitually stood on its seat, a small dog lash curled and tied into a stiff loop, and on top of the iron safe a saucer of milk.\n\n\"We don't keep a cat,\" said Miss Stoner, puzzled. \"Only the cheetah and the baboon.\"\n\n\"Ah yes. Well, a cheetah is just a big cat,\" said Holmes, \"but I fancy a saucer of milk does not go far in satisfying its wants.\"\n\nHe led us out to the lawn and gave Miss Stoner her orders. That evening she must slip across to her old room. When she heard her stepfather retire, she was to set her lamp in the window as a signal and leave the shutters undone. The rest would be in our hands.\n\n\"But what will you do?\" she asked.\n\n\"We shall spend the night in your room, in your sister's bed, and investigate the cause of these noises ourselves.\"\n\nAt dusk we watched from the Crown Inn opposite as the doctor's trap rolled home, his huge form looming over the trembling boy who drove it, his fists shaking at the gate. When a light sprang up among the trees and then went dark, Holmes rose.\n\n\"That is our signal.\"\n\nWe slipped through breaches in the park wall, crossed the lawn, and nearly lost our nerve when a hideous shape sprang from the laurels and capered away across the grass.\n\n\"My God, did you see it?\" I whispered.\n\n\"It is a nice household,\" murmured Holmes, gripping my wrist. \"That is the baboon.\"\n\nThen we were inside the bedroom with the shutters closed, sitting in absolute darkness, Holmes on the side of the bed with a long thin cane laid beside him, my revolver on the table corner. Hour after hour the parish clock boomed its quarters. A catlike whine sounded once beneath the window, the cheetah walking its rounds. Toward midnight a faint gleam showed at the ventilator and a strong smell of burning oil crept through, someone in the next room lighting a dark lantern. Then came a sound I shall never forget. Very gentle, very soothing, like a small jet of steam escaping steadily from a kettle.\n\nHolmes sprang up like a madman and struck a match, lashing furiously with his cane at the bell pull.\n\n\"You see it, Watson? You see it?\"\n\nI saw nothing. But I heard a low clear whistle, and then from behind that wall burst the most horrible shriek I ever listened to, a hoarse yell of pain and fear and anger that raised sleepers from their beds in the village below.\n\n\"It means that it is all over,\" said Holmes grimly. \"And perhaps it is for the best. Take your pistol. We will enter Dr. Roylott's room.\"\n\nThere was no answer to our knocking. We pushed open the door and stood rooted. A dark lantern threw its beam across the iron safe, and beside it, in his long gray dressing gown, sat Dr. Grimesby Roylott rigid in his chair, eyes fixed in a dreadful stare at the ceiling. Around his brow was wound a peculiar yellow band with brownish speckles.\n\n\"The band. The speckled band,\" whispered Holmes.\n\nAs we watched, the band began to move. Up through the coarse hair reared itself the squat diamond-shaped head and swollen neck of a loathsome serpent.\n\n\"It is a swamp adder,\" cried Holmes. \"The deadliest snake in India. Ten seconds after its bite a man is done for. Violence does, in truth, recoil upon the violent, and the schemer falls into the pit which he digs for another.\"\n\nWith the looped whipcord he lifted the creature at arm's length and dropped it into the iron safe, snapping the door upon it.\n\nThe whole design unfolded for us the next morning as we rode back to town. The dummy bell rope hung exactly where a climbing thing would drop from the ventilator onto a bed that was clamped to the floor so it could never be shifted from beneath the hole. The milk taught the serpent its road home. The whistle called it back each dawn before the light could betray it. Its venom left only two small punctures no coroner would mark, a poison no chemist could name, the weapon of a clever and ruthless man trained in the East. And the metallic clang Miss Stoner heard on the night her sister died was the iron door of the safe closing over the snake.\n\n\"I confess that at first I reasoned from insufficient data,\" said Holmes. \"The wanderers in the plantation, the poor girl's dying word BAND, they put me on a false scent. But whatever threatened her could come neither through door nor window, and the rest followed. Some of the blows of my cane roused the creature's temper, so that it flew upon the first person it saw. In this way I am no doubt indirectly responsible for Dr. Grimesby Roylott's death, and I cannot say that it is likely to weigh very heavily upon my conscience.\"",
