        'synopsis' => 'England\'s fastest racehorse vanishes on Dartmoor and his trainer lies dead in a hollow, and Sherlock Holmes must solve both crimes before the Wessex Cup is run.',
        'text' => "\"I am afraid, Watson, that I shall have to go,\" said Holmes one morning over breakfast.\n\n\"Go! Where to?\"\n\n\"To Dartmoor. To King's Pyland.\"\n\nI was not surprised. For days the whole of England had talked of nothing else but the disappearance of the favorite for the Wessex Cup and the murder of his trainer, and Holmes had paced our rooms in silence, deaf to every question, while fresh newspapers piled up in corners unread. Now at last he moved, and an hour later we were flying toward Exeter in a first-class carriage, he in his ear-flapped traveling cap with a bundle of papers on his knees.\n\n\"We are going well,\" said he, glancing from the window to his watch. \"Our rate at present is fifty-three and a half miles an hour.\"\n\n\"I have not observed the quarter-mile posts.\"\n\n\"Nor I. But the telegraph posts upon this line are sixty yards apart, and the calculation is a simple one.\" He tossed the last paper under the seat and lit a cigar. \"Now let me lay the facts before you, Watson, stripped of all the surmise which has smothered them.\"\n\nSilver Blaze, a bay colt with a white forehead and a mottled off-foreleg, was the most brilliant runner of his year, owned by Colonel Ross and first favorite for the Wessex Cup at odds of three to one. Enormous sums were staked upon him, and many men had the strongest interest in keeping him away from the fall of the flag. His trainer, John Straker, had served the Colonel twelve years and was known to all as zealous and honest. Each night one of three lads sat up in the stable. On the fatal Monday it was young Ned Hunter, whose supper of curried mutton was brought down by the maid, Edith Baxter, along the dark path across the moor.\n\nShe was within thirty yards of the stables when a man stepped into her lantern light, a gentlemanly figure in gray tweeds with a cloth cap and gaiters, leaning on a heavy stick. His face was pale and his manner nervous.\n\n\"'Can you tell me where I am?' he asked. 'I had almost made up my mind to sleep on the moor when I saw your lantern.'\n\n\"'You are close to the King's Pyland training stables,' said she.\n\n\"'What a stroke of luck!' he cried. 'That must be the stable-boy's supper you carry. You would not be too proud to earn the price of a new dress?' He held up a little paper packet. 'See that the boy has this tonight, and you shall have the prettiest frock that money can buy.'\n\n\"Frightened, she ran past him and told the boy through the window. The stranger came up again. 'I wanted to have a word with you,' said he, and she saw the corner of the packet protruding from his closed hand.\n\n\"'So you're one of those damned touts!' cried Hunter, springing up to loose the dog. When he rushed out, the man was gone.\"\n\n\"One moment,\" said I. \"Did the stable-boy leave the door unlocked behind him?\"\n\n\"Excellent, Watson, excellent!\" murmured Holmes. \"The point struck me so forcibly that I sent a special wire to Dartmoor yesterday about it. The boy locked the door before leaving. The window was too small for a man. Yet some hours later, Mrs. Straker woke to find her husband dressing, saying he could not sleep for anxiety about the horses. Despite the rain pattering on the window he pulled on his mackintosh and went down to the stables. At seven in the morning he had not returned. She found the stable door open, Hunter sunk in drugged stupor in his chair, the favorite's stall empty, and no sign of the trainer. A quarter mile away they came upon his overcoat hanging on a furze bush, and below it, in a bowl-shaped hollow, lay John Straker himself, his head shattered by a heavy blow, a long clean cut upon his thigh. His right hand gripped a small knife clotted with blood, and his left clasped a red and black silk cravat which the maid swore belonged to the stranger of the evening before.\"\n\n\"And the opium?\"\n\n\"Analysis showed powdered opium in what remained of the lad's supper, though the household ate the same dish without ill effect. The police have arrested the stranger, one Fitzroy Simpson, a ruined man of good birth who lived by quiet bookmaking and had registered five thousand pounds in bets against the favorite. His wet clothes showed he had been abroad in the storm, and his weighted stick might well have beaten out a man's brains.\"\n\n\"It is more than possible the wound on the thigh was made by his own knife in his dying struggle,\" I suggested.\n\n\"More than possible, probable,\" said Holmes. \"Yet the whole theory has grave objections. Why should a thief take a horse out of its locked stable? Where could a stranger hide so famous a horse? Above all, why should he drug the supper of a boy who would be no obstacle to him? No, Watson. There are difficulties here which demand a personal examination.\"\n\nWe reached Tavistock in the evening, where two men waited at the station, the tall fair Inspector Gregory with his lion-like beard and light blue eyes, and a small dapper figure with side whiskers and eyeglass, Colonel Ross himself.\n\n\"I wish to leave no stone unturned in trying to avenge poor Straker and recover my horse,\" said the Colonel.\n\nGregory talked eagerly as we drove. \"The net is drawn pretty close round Fitzroy Simpson, and I believe he is our man.\"\n\n\"A clever counsel would tear it all to rags,\" said Holmes mildly. \"Why should he lead the horse across open moor? What chemist sold him the opium? Where could he hide such a horse? But tell me, has anything new appeared?\"\n\n\"We traced a party of wandering folk who camped within a mile of the murder that Monday night and were gone by Tuesday. Perhaps the horse was led to them.\"\n\nHolmes shook his head. At the trainer's villa he examined the contents of the dead man's tin box, wax vestas, a stub of tallow candle, a briar-root pipe, a silver watch, five sovereigns, and an ivory-handled knife of the finest and most delicate blade.\n\n\"Watson, this knife is surely in your line?\"\n\n\"It is what we call a cataract knife. A very delicate blade devised for very delicate work. A strange thing to carry on a rough expedition, especially as it will not shut in the pocket.\"\n\nHe lingered over a dressmaker's bill for thirty-seven pounds fifteen shillings, made out to a Mr. William Derbyshire, and asked lightly whether Mrs. Straker had not met him once at a Plymouth garden party in a gown of dove-colored silk with ostrich feather trimming.\n\n\"I never had such a dress, sir,\" said the widow.\n\n\"Ah. That quite settles it,\" murmured Holmes.\n\nAt the scene of the crime he studied the trampled mud like a man reading print. Suddenly he plucked from it a half-burned wax match, coated in mud like a chip of wood.\n\n\"What! You expected to find it?\" cried Gregory.\n\n\"I thought it not unlikely.\" He pocketed the cast horseshoe the Inspector carried. \"And there was no wind that night, so the coat was not blown against the furze bush. It was placed there. One thing more, Colonel. Do not remove your horse's name from the entries for the Cup.\"\n\n\"Certainly not,\" cried Holmes, before the owner could speak. \"Let the name stand.\"\n\nAs we walked back over the bronze moor in the sunset he laid out his reasoning. \"A horse is a gregarious creature. If he broke loose he would go home to King's Pyland or to the rival stables at Mapleton. He is not at King's Pyland, therefore he is at Mapleton. See the value of imagination, Watson. It is the one quality Gregory lacks. We imagined what might have happened, acted on the supposition, and find ourselves justified.\" In a wet hollow the tracks of a shod horse crossed the moor, and the cast shoe from his pocket fitted exactly. Near Mapleton a man's footprints joined the horse's, turned toward King's Pyland, and swung back again to end at the gates of the rival stables themselves.\n\nAt those gates a fierce old man strode out, Silas Brown the trainer, hunting crop swinging. Holmes leaned forward and whispered a few words, and the bully flushed crimson and shouted that it was a lie. Twenty minutes later he emerged from his own parlor pale as ashes, his great hands trembling, cringing at Holmes's side.\n\n\"Your instructions shall be done. It shall all be done!\"\n\n\"There must be no mistake,\" said Holmes.\n\n\"Oh, none. None!\" And we left him guarding the apple of his eye.\n\n\"He has the horse, then?\" I cried.\n\n\"He tried to bluster, but I described his morning so exactly that he believes I watched it. He found a strange horse grazing on the moor at dawn, knew the white forehead at a glance, and saw that chance had put in his power the only animal which could beat the horse on which his money lay. So he hid it. An old faker like Brown has dodges enough to blind a searching constable. Say nothing of this to Colonel Ross. His manner has been a trifle cavalier, and I am inclined to have a little amusement at his expense.\"\n\nBack at the villa Holmes suddenly questioned a stable lad. \"You have a few sheep in the paddock. Have you noticed anything amiss with them of late?\"\n\n\"Well sir, not of much account, but three of them have gone lame.\"\n\nHolmes rubbed his hands with delight. \"A long shot, Watson, a very long shot. Gregory, let me recommend to your attention this singular epidemic among the sheep.\"\n\n\"You consider that important?\"\n\n\"Exceedingly so.\"\n\n\"Is there any point to which you would wish to draw my attention?\"\n\n\"To the curious incident of the dog in the nighttime.\"\n\n\"The dog did nothing in the nighttime.\"\n\n\"That was the curious incident,\" remarked Sherlock Holmes.\n\nFour days later we stood beside Colonel Ross's drag at the Winchester racecourse, and never was a man colder. \"I have seen nothing of my horse,\" said he.\n\n\"I suppose you would know him when you saw him?\"\n\n\"I have been on the turf for twenty years, sir, and never was asked such a question. A child would know Silver Blaze by his white forehead and his mottled off-foreleg.\"\n\nThe bell rang, six horses swept past, and among them ran a powerful bay without one white hair upon his body. Yet when the straight was run it was that nameless bay which passed the post six lengths before Lord Backwater's Desborough, while the ring roared that the unknown was the shortest-priced winner ever seen.\n\n\"Now, Colonel, you shall know everything,\" said Holmes. We walked into the weighing enclosure. \"You have only to wash his face and his leg in spirits of wine, and you will find he is the same old Silver Blaze as ever.\"\n\n\"You take my breath away!\"\n\n\"I found him in the hands of a faker, and took the liberty of running him just as he stood. And since you ask me to lay hands on the murderer of John Straker, I have done so. He is standing immediately behind you.\"\n\nThe Colonel swung round angrily. Then Holmes stepped past him and laid his hand upon the glossy neck of the thoroughbred.\n\n\"The horse!\" we cried together.\n\n\"Yes, the horse. It may lessen his guilt that it was done in self-defense, and that John Straker was a man who was entirely unworthy of your confidence. But there goes the bell, and as I stand to win a little on this next race, I shall defer the explanation until a fitting time.\"\n\nIn the corner of a Pullman car that evening he gave it us entire. \"The curried mutton was the first link in my chain. Powdered opium is by no means tasteless, yet only a strongly flavored dish could disguise it. By no possibility could the stranger Simpson have caused curry to be served that night in the trainer's kitchen. Therefore the drug was put in by someone inside that house, after the dish was set aside for the boy alone. Then I remembered the silence of the dog. A hound was kept in the yard, yet the midnight visitor who fetched out the famous horse roused neither bark nor sleeper. Obviously the visitor was someone whom the dog knew well.\"\n\n\"Straker himself!\" cried the Colonel.\n\n\"Himself, down in the dark with dishonest purpose. For what purpose? Look at the knife. A surgeon's instrument for the most delicate operations. There is a trick by which a racing man may make a tiny nick in the tendons of a horse's ham beneath the skin, leaving absolutely no trace. The beast goes lame forever, and the world blames a strain. Your trainer meant to lame your champion on the moor, having bet against him through agents, and he practiced first upon your sheep.\"\n\n\"Villain! Scoundrel!\"\n\n\"In the hollow he slipped off his coat, struck a match behind the horse, and the creature, feeling mischief intended, lashed out with all its spirit. The steel shoe caught him full upon the forehead, and as he fell his own knife gashed his thigh. He had picked up the cravat Simpson dropped in flight, perhaps to bind the horse's leg, and so it was found in his dead hand. As for motive, I called upon the Bond Street milliner, who knows her customer Derbyshire very well indeed. A dashing lady with a taste for twenty-guinea gowns had plunged the poor fool head over ears in debt, and led him to this miserable plot.\"\n\n\"Wonderful!\" cried the Colonel. \"You might have been there!\"\n\n\"And now,\" said Holmes, smiling faintly as the lamps of London flickered past, \"there remains only to arrange a little amnesty with our friend Silas Brown, for the sake of a certain horse who has had quite adventures enough for one week.\"",
