        'cover' => 'covers/mystery-final-problem.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'Sherlock Holmes faces the one man whose intellect matches his own, a hidden professor who rules half the crime of London, and their duel ends beside a Swiss abyss.',
        'text' => "It is with a heavy heart that I take up my pen, for I am about to write of the event which created a void in my life that two years have done little to fill. My hand was forced at last by letters in which Colonel James Moriarty defended the memory of his brother, for I alone know the absolute truth of what passed between Professor Moriarty and Mr. Sherlock Holmes.\n\nI had married and left Baker Street by then, and saw my old friend only three times in 1890. So it was with surprise that I looked up one evening in April 1891 to find him in my consulting room, paler and thinner than usual.\n\n\"Yes, I have been using myself up rather too freely,\" said he, answering my look rather than my words. \"Have you any objection to closing your shutters?\"\n\nHe bolted them himself, then sat smoking with his face drawn tight.\n\n\"You are afraid of something?\"\n\n\"Well, I am.\"\n\n\"Of what?\"\n\n\"Of air-guns.\" He smiled at my astonishment and held out his hand. Two knuckles were burst and bleeding. \"It is not an airy nothing, you see. It is solid enough for a man to break his hand over. And I must beg you to be so unconventional as to let me leave presently by scrambling over your back garden wall.\"\n\nThen he explained, fingertips together, elbows on knees.\n\n\"You have probably never heard of Professor Moriarty?\"\n\n\"Never.\"\n\n\"Aye, there is the genius and the wonder of the thing! The man pervades London, and no one has heard of him. He is the Napoleon of crime, Watson. The organizer of half that is evil and nearly all that is undetected in this great city. A philosopher, an abstract thinker, a brain of the first order. He sits motionless, like a spider in the center of its web, but that web has a thousand radiations, and he knows every quiver of each of them. At twenty-one he wrote a treatise upon the binomial theorem which won him a mathematical chair, but a criminal strain ran in his blood, sharpened instead of checked by his mighty powers. Dark rumors drove him down to London, where he set up as an army coach. Behind him stands nothing less than a syndicate, Watson, and for years I have felt its shield thrown over every wrongdoer I hunted. Three months ago I seized my thread and followed it through a thousand cunning windings to him. I confess I had at last met an antagonist who was my intellectual equal. My horror at his crimes was lost in admiration of his skill. But the net is woven round him now, and on Monday next he and all the principal members of his gang will be in the hands of the police.\"\n\n\"And he knows this?\"\n\n\"This very morning he stood in my own doorway.\" Holmes's eyes were grim at the memory. \"A very tall man, Watson, with a forehead that domes out in a white curve, sunken eyes, rounded shoulders, and a face forever oscillating slowly from side to side in a curiously reptilian fashion. I had my revolver out of the drawer before he crossed the threshold, and I covered him through the cloth of my dressing gown. You have less frontal development than I should have expected, said he. It is a dangerous habit to finger loaded firearms in the pocket of one's dressing gown.\n\n\"I laid the pistol on the table. All that I have to say has already crossed your mind, said he.\n\n\"Then possibly my answer has crossed yours.\n\n\"You stand fast?\n\n\"Absolutely.\n\n\"He took out a memorandum book and read me dates as a schoolmaster reads a lesson. You crossed my path on the fourth of January. On the twenty-third you incommoded me. By February I was seriously inconvenienced. By the end of March absolutely hampered. And now I am in positive danger of losing my liberty. You must drop it, Mr. Holmes. You really must, you know.\n\n\"After Monday, said I.\n\n\"He shook his head sadly. Danger is part of my trade, I told him.\n\n\"That is not danger. It is inevitable destruction. You stand in the way not merely of an individual, but of a mighty organization, the full extent of which you have been unable to realize. You must stand clear, Mr. Holmes, or be trodden under foot.\n\n\"At the door he turned. It has been a duel between you and me, Mr. Holmes. You hope to place me in the dock. I tell you that I will never stand in the dock. You hope to beat me. I tell you that you will never beat me. If you are clever enough to bring destruction upon me, rest assured that I shall do as much to you.\"\n\nHolmes had not waited idle since. A two-horse van had whizzed at him on the Welbeck Street crossing, missing him by a fraction of a second. A brick had crashed at his feet from a roof in Vere Street. And that very evening a ruffian with a bludgeon had jumped him in Kensington, to the lasting injury of the man's front teeth and Holmes's knuckles. No thread would ever connect them to the retired mathematical coach working out problems ten miles away.\n\n\"So I ask you, Watson, to come away with me to the Continent tomorrow. Obey my instructions to the letter, for we are playing a double-handed game against the cleverest rogue and the most powerful syndicate of criminals in Europe. Send your luggage unaddressed tonight. In the morning take neither the first nor the second hansom, drive to the Lowther Arcade, dash through it on foot, and enter the small brougham waiting at the far curb, driven by a fellow in a dark cloak tipped with red. The second first-class carriage from the front will be reserved for us at Victoria.\"\n\nThe cloak belonged to no hired servant but to his brother Mycroft, as I learned later. As for Holmes, I found him already aboard when the whistle blew, disguised as a decrepit Italian priest, and I confess I had helped the old gentleman into my reserved carriage myself without a glimmer of suspicion. The wrinkles smoothed away, the dull eyes regained their fire, and Holmes laughed at my amazement. Then he grew grave and pointed back through the window glass.\n\n\"There is Moriarty himself.\"\n\nOn the receding platform a tall man was pushing furiously through the crowd, waving his hand as if he would stop the train itself.\n\n\"They set fire to our rooms in Baker Street last night,\" said Holmes quietly. \"No great harm was done. But he will follow us, Watson. If our positions were reversed I should engage a special train, and he will do exactly what I should do. This express stops at Canterbury. We shall get out there.\"\n\nWe stood behind a pile of luggage and watched our own train come thundering through Canterbury without pausing, rocking and swinging over the points into the Kentish darkness.\n\n\"There he goes,\" said Holmes. \"There are limits, you see, even to our friend's intelligence.\"\n\nBy devious roads we traveled that week, Newhaven, Dieppe, Brussels, and so toward Switzerland, while in London the net closed. At Strasbourg came a telegram which Holmes tore open and hurled into the grate with a bitter curse.\n\n\"I might have known it! They have secured the whole gang except him. He has given them the slip. Watson, you had better return to England, for I shall be a dangerous companion now. His occupation is gone. He will devote his whole energies to revenge.\"\n\nIt was an appeal wasted on an old campaigner. That same night we were bound for Geneva, and for a charming week we wandered up the valley of the Rhone and over the snow-deep Gemmi Pass to Meiringen. Yet never for one instant did Holmes forget the shadow. When a rock clattered down from the ridge above the lonely Daubensee behind us, he was up the hillside in an instant, scanning every pinnacle. And yet he was never depressed. Rather I never knew him in such exuberant spirits.\n\n\"I think that I may go so far as to say, Watson, that I have not lived wholly in vain,\" he said once among the mountains. \"If my record were closed tonight I could survey it with equanimity. The air of London is the sweeter for my presence.\"\n\nOn the afternoon of the fourth of May we left the little village of Meiringen to cross the hills toward Rosenlaui, with strict instructions not to pass the falls of Reichenbach without stopping to admire them. It is indeed a fearful place. The torrent, swollen by melting snow, plunges into a tremendous abyss lined with glistening coal-black rock, and the spray rolls up like the smoke from a burning house while a half-human shout comes booming out of the chasm.\n\nWe had turned to retrace our steps when a Swiss lad came running along the path with a letter bearing the mark of our hotel. An English lady, it said, had arrived within minutes of our leaving, stricken in the last stage of consumption, dying, and begging for the consolation of an English doctor.\n\nI could not refuse such a call. It was agreed that Holmes should keep the lad as guide and follow me over the hill to Rosenlaui in the evening. As I turned away I saw him standing with his back against the rock and his arms folded, gazing down at the rush of the waters. It was the last I was ever destined to see of him in this world. And once, looking back from below the shoulder of the hill, I marked a tall black figure walking very rapidly along the path above me. Then I thought no more of it.\n\nOld Steiler met me at his hotel door with blank astonishment on his face. There was no sick Englishwoman. There had never been any letter but the one I carried, written after our departure by a tall Englishman who had called while we were out.\n\nI ran. Two hours of frantic climbing brought me back to the falls. Holmes's alpenstock still leaned against the rock where I had left him, but of Holmes there was no sign, and my shouting only rolled back at me in echoes. On that soft black soil kept forever moist by the spray, two lines of footprints ran side by side toward the broken end of the path, and there were none returning. A few yards on the earth was plowed into mud, and the branches fringing the chasm hung torn and bedraggled. I lay flat and peered down until giddiness took me, and only the gleam of broken water answered from the depths.\n\nFrom the top of the jutting rock flashed something bright. His silver cigarette case. Beneath it lay three pages torn from his notebook, the writing as firm and clear as if penned in Baker Street.\n\n\"My dear Watson, I write these few lines through the courtesy of Mr. Moriarty, who awaits my convenience for the final discussion of those questions which lie between us. He has been giving me a sketch of the methods by which he avoided the English police and kept himself informed of our movements, and they confirm the very high opinion which I had formed of his abilities. I am pleased to think that I shall be able to free society from any further effects of his presence, though I fear it is at a cost which will give pain to my friends, and especially, my dear Watson, to you. Tell Inspector Patterson that the papers which he needs to convict the gang are in pigeonhole M., done up in a blue envelope and inscribed Moriarty. Pray give my greetings to Mrs. Watson, and believe me to be, my dear fellow,\n\n\"Very sincerely yours,\n\n\"Sherlock Holmes.\"\n\nExperts found little doubt in the end. A personal contest between two men ended, as it could hardly fail to end in so narrow a place, with both reeling over the edge locked in each other's arms, and deep in that dreadful cauldron of swirling water and seething foam will lie forever the most dangerous criminal and the foremost champion of the law of their generation. Of the gang, the blue envelope destroyed them utterly.\n\nBut why should I prolong the account? Why should I dwell on the empty chair, the untouched chemical apparatus, the long silence of Baker Street? Let me say only what I have earned the right to say, who knew him best of living men. He was, and remains in my heart, the best and the wisest man whom I have ever known.",
