        'synopsis' => 'Paris\'s greatest detective hunts the tallest criminal alive across London by following a trail of absurdities, and finds that the trail was laid on purpose by the last man he ever suspected.',
        'text' => "Between the silver ribbon of morning and the green glittering ribbon of sea, the boat touched Harwich and let loose a swarm of folk like flies, among whom the man we must follow was by no means conspicuous. There was nothing notable about him except a slight contrast between the holiday gaiety of his clothes and the official gravity of his face. Nothing indicated that the gray jacket covered a loaded revolver, that the white waistcoat covered a police card, or that the straw hat covered one of the most powerful intellects in Europe. For this was Valentin himself, head of the Paris police, and he had come from Brussels to London to make the greatest arrest of the century.\n\nFlambeau was in England.\n\nOf this colossus of crime it is enough to say that for years almost every morning paper announced how he had escaped the consequences of one extraordinary crime by committing another. He was a Gascon of gigantic stature and bodily daring who could run down the Rue de Rivoli with a policeman under each arm. His real crimes were ingenious wholesale robberies, each almost a new sin. It was he who ran the great dairy company in London with no dairies, no cows, and no milk, serving a thousand subscribers by simply moving other people's milk cans to the doors of his own customers. He could leap like a grasshopper and melt into treetops like a monkey.\n\nBut how find him? Flambeau's disguises could not hide one thing, his height, four inches above six feet. So when Valentin boarded the train from Harwich he studied every passenger for that one mark, and found nobody but a very short Roman Catholic priest going up from a small Essex village. The little priest was so much the essence of those eastern flats that Valentin almost laughed aloud. He had a face as round and dull as a Norfolk dumpling, eyes as empty as the North Sea, and several brown paper parcels which he was quite incapable of collecting. He dropped his shabby umbrella on the floor, did not seem to know which end of his return ticket was right, and explained to everybody in the carriage that he must be careful because he carried something made of real silver with blue stones. Valentin even warned him kindly not to tell strangers such things, and thought no more about him forever.\n\nIn London beyond Victoria the detective paused before a quiet square and a restaurant that looked as if it had strayed from Soho, with lemon-yellow blinds and a flight of steps running up like a fire escape. He went in on pure impulse, for his method in darkness was a strange one. When he could not follow the train of the reasonable, he coldly followed the train of the unreasonable, knocking at empty houses and turning down blocked lanes on the chance that any oddity which caught HIS eye might be the same oddity that caught the eye of the pursued.\n\nOver his black coffee he mused sourly that the criminal is the creative artist while the detective is only the critic. Then he lifted his cup, put it down very quickly. He had put salt in it. He examined the table. The sugar basin held salt, the salt cellars held sugar. Except for an odd dark splash on the white papered wall, all else seemed neat and ordinary.\n\n\"Do you play this delicate joke on your customers every morning?\" he asked the waiter. \"Does changing the salt and sugar never pall?\"\n\nThe bewildered man summoned his proprietor, and between them the truth came stumbling out. Two clergymen had eaten soup there at opening time. One paid and went out. The slower one, gathering his things, deliberately picked up his half-empty cup at the door and threw the soup slap against the wall. They went round the corner into Carstairs Street.\n\nValentin was on his feet with his hat settled and stick in hand. In universal darkness he could follow only the first odd finger that pointed, and this finger was odd enough.\n\nThat same finger kept pointing all day. In a greengrocer's window the price cards had been swapped, Best tangerine oranges two a penny stuck upon the Brazil nuts, Finest Brazil nuts fourpence a pound upon the oranges, and the furious shopman reported how one of the parsons had rolled all his apples into the street. On a yellow omnibus grinding northward through thirteen separate vulgar cities touching end to end, Valentin told his two borrowed policemen his creed. If you know what a man is doing, get in front of him. But if you want to GUESS what he is doing, keep behind him. Stray when he strays, stop when he stops, travel as slowly as he, and keep your eyes skinned for an odd thing.\n\nThe odd thing appeared at Camden Town, where a frosted window of a palatial public house bore a big black smash like a star in the ice. Inside, the waiter told an even stranger tale. Two foreign parsons lunched quietly. One paid and left. The other, caught at the door, admitted paying fourteen shillings on a bill of four.\n\n\"'Sorry to confuse your accounts,' says he serene enough, 'but it'll pay for the window.' 'What window?' says I. 'The one I'm going to break,' says he, and smashed that blessed pane with his umbrella.\"\n\n\"Are we after escaped lunatics?\" muttered the inspector under his breath.\n\nThen Bullock Street, bare brick ways like tunnels, dusk deepening, until Valentin stopped before a garish sweetshop and bought thirteen chocolate cigars with entire gravity. The angular woman at the counter woke at sight of the blue uniform.\n\n\"If you've come about that parcel, I've sent it off already.\"\n\n\"What parcel?\" said Valentin, and it was his turn to look blank.\n\n\"The clergyman gentleman's. He ran back asking had he left a parcel, and I found none. But when he'd gone again I found he HAD, so I posted it to the address he gave me, somewhere in Westminster.\"\n\nStraight on fifteen minutes lay the open heath, a dome of peacock green sinking into gold above the Vale of Health. And there across the valley Valentin saw the thing he sought, two clerical figures seated under a tree on the wilder heights, one small and round, one well over six feet tall.\n\nSo the whole plot stood plain at last. Father Brown of Essex was bringing a silver cross set with sapphires to the Congress. Flambeau had heard of it, for Flambeau heard of everything. Dressed as a second priest he had led this helpless sheep across London toward some lonely theft. Yet when Valentin thought of splashed wallpaper and swapped fruit labels and windows paid for before they were broken, he racked his brains for any rhyme or reason in it. He had grasped the criminal, but still he could not grasp the clue.\n\nCreeping close behind the great branching tree, he heard the two priests talking exactly like priests, piously and at leisure, of whether other worlds might rise higher than our reason.\n\n\"No,\" said the little priest, turning sharply in his seat. \"Reason is always reasonable, even in the lost borderland of things. Alone on earth the Church affirms that God himself is bound by reason. Look at those stars. You may think them forests of adamant with leaves of brilliants, you may fancy the moon a single elephantine sapphire, but don't imagine that all that frantic astronomy would make the smallest difference to reason and justice of conduct. On plains of opal, under cliffs cut out of pearl, you would still find a notice board. THOU SHALT NOT STEAL.\"\n\n\"I think,\" said the tall priest mildly, with his head bowed and his hands on his knees, \"that other worlds may perhaps rise higher than our reason.\" Then, without changing his voice by the faintest shade, he added, \"Just hand over that sapphire cross of yours, will you? We are all alone here, and I could pull you to pieces like a straw doll.\"\n\nThe little priest turned a dazed face in the dusk. \"Are you sure?\"\n\nFlambeau yelled with delight. \"Yes, you turnip, I am quite sure. I made a duplicate of the right parcel. You carry the duplicate and I carry the jewels. An old dodge, Father Brown. A very old dodge.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Father Brown vaguely. \"I have heard of it before.\"\n\n\"You have? Where?\"\n\n\"Well, I mustn't tell you his name. He was a penitent, you know. Twenty years he lived entirely on duplicate brown paper parcels. When I began to suspect you I remembered his way of doing things.\"\n\n\"Suspect me! Because I brought you up this bare heath?\"\n\n\"No. I suspected you when we first met. It is that little bulge up the sleeve where you people wear the spiked bracelet.\"\n\n\"How in Tartarus did you ever hear of the spiked bracelet?\"\n\n\"Oh, one's little flock, you know. When I was a curate in Hartlepool there were three of them with spiked bracelets. So I watched you change the parcels, changed them back again myself, and then left the right one behind. At that sweetshop I asked if I had left a parcel and gave an address if it should turn up. I knew I had left none, but when I went away again I had. Instead of running after me with the valuable one, they have posted it flying to a friend of mine in Westminster.\"\n\nFlambeau tore the parcel from his breast pocket and rent it in pieces. Nothing but paper and sticks of lead. He sprang up with a gigantic gesture.\n\n\"I don't believe a bumpkin like you managed all that. We are all alone, and I'll take it by force!\"\n\n\"No,\" said Father Brown, standing up also. \"You won't take it by force. First, because I really haven't got it. And second, because we are not alone. Behind that tree are two strong policemen and the greatest detective alive. How did they come here? Why, I brought them, of course!\"\n\nThe world seemed to wait for Flambeau to leap like a tiger, but he was held spellbound by sheer curiosity.\n\n\"I wasn't sure you were a thief, you see, and it would never do to make a scandal against one of our own clergy. So I tested you. A man generally makes a scene if he finds salt in his coffee. If he keeps quiet, he has a reason for quietness. I changed the salt and sugar, and you kept quiet. A man generally objects if his bill comes three times too big. If he pays, he wants to pass unnoticed. I altered your bill, and you paid it. But since YOU would never leave tracks for the police, somebody had to. At every place we stopped I took care to do something that would get us talked about for the rest of the day. A splashed wall. Spilled apples. A broken window. Not much harm done, but the cross is saved, and saved it always shall be.\"\n\n\"But how in blazes do you know all these horrors?\" cried the thief.\n\nA shadow of a smile crossed the round simple face.\n\n\"Oh, by being a celibate simpleton, I suppose,\" said Father Brown. \"Has it never struck you that a man who does next to nothing but hear men's real sins is not likely to be wholly unaware of human evil? And besides, another part of my trade told me you were no priest.\"\n\n\"What part?\"\n\n\"You attacked reason,\" said Father Brown. \"It is bad theology.\"\n\nAnd even as he turned away to collect his property the three policemen came out from under the twilight trees. Flambeau was an artist and a sportsman to the last. He stepped back and swept Valentin a great bow.\n\n\"Do not bow to me, mon ami,\" said Valentin with silver clearness. \"Let us both bow to our master.\"\n\nAnd they stood an instant uncovered, while the little Essex priest blinked about him for his umbrella.",
