        'cover' => 'covers/classic-around-world.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'An unshakably precise English gentleman wagers half his fortune that he can circle the globe in eighty days, and races trains, elephants, typhoons, and one relentless detective to win it.',
        'text' => "In 1872 Mr. Phileas Fogg lived at No. 7 Saville Row, the house in which Sheridan died. He was one of the most noticeable members of the Reform Club, though he seemed always to avoid attention, an enigmatical personage about whom little was known except that he was a polished man of the world. People said he resembled Byron, at least his head did, but he was a bearded, tranquil Byron who might live a thousand years without growing old. He was certainly rich, though none could say how he had made his fortune, and he belonged to no society whatever. He belonged to the Reform, and that was all.\n\nHis habits were known to the minute. At eleven twenty-five he stepped into his house, ate breakfast and tea exactly at the same hours, shaved at thirty-seven minutes past eleven by his chronometer which never varied a second, and at midnight he slept, his apartment lit by the scientific gas burner which burned precisely as much gas as was needed.\n\nOn this October morning he had dismissed his valet for bringing water two degrees too cold, eighty-four degrees instead of eighty-six, and was expecting a new servant between eleven and half past. At eleven twenty-nine appeared a young fellow of thirty, ruddy-faced, with a look honest and good-natured enough. He was a Frenchman named Jean, called Passepartout, who had wandered through every trade, singer, circus rider, fireman on the fastest express in Paris, and had come to England hoping at last for a quiet life in some respectable family.\n\n\"You are recommended?\" asked Mr. Fogg. \"You know my conditions?\"\n\n\"Yes, monsieur.\"\n\n\"Good. What time do you have?\"\n\n\"Twenty-two minutes after eleven,\" replied Passepartout, drawing an enormous silver watch from deep in his waistband.\n\n\"You are too slow. Have you any objection to being engaged in general use?\"\n\n\"None whatever. But I must say, monsieur, that I have come to seek repose, and I hear you are a gentleman of the most exact regularity.\"\n\n\"Exactness is the soul of things,\" said Mr. Fogg tranquilly, and so master and man accepted each other, the one as master, the other as man.\n\nThat evening Phileas Fogg played whist at the Reform Club with his usual partners, and the talk fell upon the great robbery lately done at the Bank of England, fifty-five thousand pounds carried off from the cashier's desk in broad daylight. Where could the thief fly?\n\n\"The world is big enough,\" said Stuart.\n\n\"It was once,\" said Phileas Fogg in a low tone. \"Cut, sir.\"\n\n\"What do you mean by once? Has the world grown smaller?\"\n\n\"Certainly,\" returned Ralph. \"A man can now go round it ten times more quickly than a hundred years ago.\"\n\n\"In eighty days,\" interrupted Fogg, and Sullivan read aloud the itinerary printed in the Morning Chronicle. Seven days London to Suez, thirteen Suez to Bombay, three by rail to Calcutta, thirteen to Hong Kong, six to Yokohama, twenty-two across the Pacific, seven by rail to New York, nine home again. Eighty days total.\n\n\"But that takes no account of bad weather, contrary winds, shipwrecks, railway accidents!\"\n\n\"All included,\" said Fogg quietly, studying his cards.\n\n\"Theoretically, Mr. Fogg, but practically!\"\n\n\"Practically also, Mr. Stuart.\"\n\n\"I would like to see you do it in eighty days.\"\n\n\"It depends on you. Shall we go?\"\n\n\"Heaven preserve me! But I will wager four thousand pounds that such a journey is impossible.\"\n\n\"Quite possible, on the contrary.\"\n\n\"Well then, make it!\"\n\n\"The journey round the world in eighty days?\"\n\n\"At once,\" said Phileas Fogg. \"Only I warn you I shall do it at your expense.\" And when Stuart cried that he meant it, Fogg rose. \"I have a deposit of twenty thousand pounds at Baring's. I will willingly risk it. Today is Wednesday, October the second. I shall be due back in this very room, Saturday, December the twenty-first, at a quarter before nine in the evening, or else the twenty thousand belong to you in fact and in right. Here is my check.\"\n\n\"I am quite ready now,\" said he when the clock struck seven. \"Diamonds are trumps. Be so good as to play, gentlemen.\"\n\nHe won the rubber, gave his twenty guineas of winnings to a beggar woman at Charing Cross station, told the astonished Passepartout that they were going round the world in eighty days with no luggage but a carpet bag, and boarded the Dover train at eight forty-five. In the bag lay twenty thousand pounds in banknotes.\n\nNow it chanced that a warrant was already out for a certain robber, and Detective Fix, sent down to Suez, stood watching the passengers come ashore, thinking how easily a man might flee justice aboard a fast steamer. When Mr. Fogg landed, presented a passport for visa, and calmly asked whether English visas were still needed in India, Fix's heart leaped. This was his man. The face matched the description. Only the warrant must come out from London to make an arrest abroad, and so Fix appointed himself the traveler's shadow for eighty days, swearing inwardly to hold him until it arrived.\n\nAt Bombay the party should have caught a train at eight, but Passepartout, wandering into a pagoda against all rules, escaped its priests bareheaded and shoeless, and the train went out without them, losing two days. There followed the strangest stretch of the whole journey, for the Great Indian Peninsula Railway stopped short beyond Kholby, though no newspaper had said so, and Mr. Fogg hired an elephant named Kiouni from a young Indian guide at two thousand pounds' worth of persuasion, riding through jungle and bamboo forest toward Allahabad.\n\nIn the depths of the forest they heard drums and trumpets, and saw a sad procession moving to a temple, Brahmans leading, musicians blowing, behind them an old rajah's corpse borne on a bier and beside it a young woman fair as a European, led stumbling along. It was a widow going to be burned alive on her husband's pyre at dawn.\n\n\"They shall not,\" said Phileas Fogg, when the guide explained.\n\nRescue by force was hopeless against a hundred armed men. So they waited till night, crept near the pyre where the victim lay drugged beside the body, and while Fogg held his revolver ready to shield their flight, Passepartout slipped through the smoke and shadows among the watchers. The flames were kindled. The crowd fell back. And suddenly the dead rajah himself rose up from the burning bed, lifted his widow in his arms, and descended from the pyre alive, while priests and people flung themselves flat in holy terror. It was only Passepartout, who had lain down in place of the corpse, but never was resurrection more useful. The next day Aouda, grateful and orphaned, joined the journey, and Mr. Fogg spent a fortune getting her safe to Hong Kong.\n\nThenceforth misfortune hunted them like the detective at their heels. At Hong Kong Fix, fearing to lose his prisoner when the voyage turned homeward, lured Passepartout into a smoking house and left him senseless, so that the Frenchman missed the steamer and believed for days his master had abandoned him. Fogg missed the Carnatic too, but chartered a little pilot boat, the Tankadere, and beat her through a gale to Shanghai, catching the Yokohama mail steamer as she blew her first whistle. At San Francisco a street election battle swallowed them. On the plains the train halted three hours behind ten thousand buffalo marching across the track like a living rampart. Near Medicine Bow the rails ahead rested on a rotten bridge, and the engineer drove the train over it at full speed, the whole span crashing into the river the instant the last car touched land. And past Fort Kearney a band of Sioux swarmed the train, carrying off Passepartout, until Mr. Fogg, offering his fortune for soldiers, rode to the rescue and brought his man back, having simply done his duty.\n\nNew York was reached too late for the China, and so Mr. Fogg chartered an ancient steamer, the Henrietta, and when the coal ran out he purchased the vessel outright and fed her wooden fittings piecemeal into her own furnace fires, arriving at length on Irish shores with scarcely a spar remaining.\n\nAt Liverpool, on the morning of December the twenty-first, with nine hours to spare, a hand fell on his shoulder.\n\n\"Phileas Fogg,\" said Detective Fix at last, \"I arrest you in the Queen's name!\"\n\nThree hours later the true robber, one James Strang, having been taken in Edinburgh weeks before, the innocent traveler walked free. But the train was lost. He reached London at ten minutes to nine that evening, five minutes late, and went silently home, ruined. He owed his friends nothing now but apology, and gave it them.\n\nThat night Miss Aouda asked, trembling, whether the disaster had made life unbearable to him, and offered herself, since his ruin was partly hers. He answered gravely that he wished to ask HER that question. She blushed. The next day, while Passepartout ran joyful to engage the Reverend Samuel Wilson for the wedding, Mr. Fogg sat alone in Saville Row awaiting ruin with perfect calm.\n\nThen Passepartout came flying back hatless, hair wild, bursting into the room.\n\n\"Master! Marriage impossible! Impossible for tomorrow!\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because tomorrow is Sunday!\"\n\n\"Monday,\" said Mr. Fogg.\n\n\"No! Today is Saturday. We arrived twenty-four hours early! You have made a mistake of one day, and there are only TEN MINUTES left!\"\n\nAnd seizing his master by the collar, the Frenchman dragged him downstairs and into a cab, promising the driver a hundred pounds, running over two dogs and upsetting five carriages, until the doors of the Reform Club opened before the clock struck quarter to nine.\n\nFor Phileas Fogg, traveling ever eastward toward the sun, had seen it cross the meridian eighty times while London saw it only seventy-nine. He had gained a day without knowing it, a whole four and twenty hours, and those very hours stood between him and beggary.\n\nAt fifty-seven seconds before the stroke of nine, Phileas Fogg appeared in the great saloon and said in his calm voice.\n\n\"Here I am, gentlemen.\"\n\nHe had won. Of the stake, nearly nineteen thousand had gone into steamers, railways, sledges, elephants, and one burned ship, yet what matter. He divided what remained between his servant and even poor Fix, deducting from Passepartout's share only the cost of the gas which had burned nineteen hundred hours for nothing, for the sake of regularity. And that evening he asked Miss Aouda whether their marriage still suited her, and she answered that it was for her to ask, since yesterday he had been poor and today was rich again, and he answered, Dear Aouda, and she answered, Dear Mr. Fogg, and there was nothing more to settle.\n\nBut Passepartout had one more marvel. \"Do you know, sir,\" said he next morning, \"we could have gone round the world in seventy-eight days!\"\n\n\"No doubt,\" returned Phileas Fogg, \"by not crossing India. But if I had not crossed India, I should never have saved Aouda, and she would never have become my wife.\"\n\nAnd he shut the door very gently.\n\nWhat had he truly gained by his trouble? Nothing, say you? Perhaps so. Nothing but a charming woman who, strange as it may seem, made him the happiest of men. And truly, would you not for less than that make the tour of the world?",
