    [
        'slug' => 'classic-leagues-under-sea',
        'gutenberg_id' => 164,
        'title' => 'Twenty Thousand Leagues Under the Sea',
        'author' => 'Jules Verne',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'genres' => ['Science Fiction'],
        'quiz_difficulty' => 'medium',
        'cover' => 'covers/classic-leagues-under-sea.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'A professor captured by a mysterious captain aboard a marvel submarine tours the wonders and terrors of the deep, until he learns that his host is a man at war with the whole surface world.',
        'text' => "The year 1866 was signalized by a strange event which no one has yet forgotten. Sailors of every nation reported meeting an enormous thing in the sea, a long slender object sometimes phosphorescent, many times larger than a whale, which moved with unbelievable speed. The journals discussed it, the academies debated it, ships had been struck by it as if by a blade. At last, when a steamer was pierced in her hull three feet below the waterline, public opinion declared that a gigantic narwhal infested the high seas, and great vessels were dispatched to hunt it down.\n\nI, Pierre Aronnax, professor in the Museum of Paris, returning from Nebraska where I had been studying prehistoric remains, arrived in New York just as the frigate Abraham Lincoln prepared to sail in pursuit of this monster, and my own article on the question made them beg me aboard. With me came my devoted servant Conseil, a boy who could classify every creature alive but never remember what anything was called, and who followed me everywhere saying, If master wishes.\n\nFor months we scoured the Pacific. The only man aboard who doubted the unicorn theory was Ned Land, a Canadian harpooner with hands like iron, who growled that no such animal existed. One night his doubts were answered in the worst way. The frigate closed upon the luminous beast, cannonballs glanced off its hide as from sheet iron, and when Ned flung his harpoon it rang against some hard body and fell into the sea. Then a shock threw us all to the deck, and the frigate, disabled, drifted away while the sea swallowed three of her passengers, Ned Land, Conseil, and myself, onto the very back of the supposed narwhal.\n\nIt was steel beneath our feet, not flesh. A hatch opened, eight masked men drew us down, and thus began our captivity aboard the strangest vessel ever built, the Nautilus.\n\nHer commander came to us at last, a self-confident man of majestic calm, thirty-five to fifty years of age perhaps, with fine dark eyes and a look of energy that seemed made to dominate. He spoke French perfectly, English, German, Latin, offering each tongue in turn, and he named himself simply Captain Nemo, and his vessel Mobilis in Mobili, mobile in the mobile element.\n\n\"You violate no doubt the rights of men by keeping us prisoners,\" I said boldly.\n\n\"Professor, I will answer you once for all. Whoever enters the Nautilus must never leave it. You are guests, not prisoners, and if you give me your word you shall be free aboard. But understand, no one leaves.\"\n\n\"Absolute submission to your commands, then?\"\n\n\"Not at all. But I warn you, here you will find everything, and outside nothing can reach you. This ship is a sanctuary. And now, Monsieur Aronnax, permit me to lead the way. Breakfast awaits us.\"\n\nWhat a breakfast! Dishes unknown to science, all furnished by the sea alone, fillet of turtle dressed as meat, dolphins' liver taken for pork ragout, cream whose milk came from whales and whose sugar from North Sea kelp, preserves of sea cucumber and anemone that a Malay would envy. When I asked how such a table could exist far from land, the captain's eyes kindled.\n\n\"Yes, Professor, I love it. The sea is everything. It covers seven tenths of the terrestrial globe. Its breath is pure and healthy. It is an immense desert where man is never lonely, for he feels life stirring on all sides. The globe began with the sea, and who knows but it will end with it? In it is supreme tranquility. The sea does not belong to despots. Upon its surface men may still exercise unjust laws, fight, tear one another to pieces. But at thirty feet below its level their reign ceases, their power vanishes. Ah, sir, live, live in the heart of the waters. There only is independence. There I recognize no masters. There I am free!\"\n\n\"And you have broken with humanity entirely?\"\n\n\"I am not what you call a civilized man, Professor. I have done with society entirely, for reasons which I alone have the right to appreciate. I do not therefore obey its laws. I ask you never to speak to me again of things past. As for your books, my library holds twelve thousand volumes. My salon holds paintings by the masters and the last gifts of the ocean itself, pearls and coral and shells without price. Electricity gives us light, heat, motion. The Nautilus wants nothing. She is a perfect world.\"\n\nSo began ten months of wonders such as no traveler had ever dreamed. We glided through the warm currents watching through crystal windows while sharks pursued flying fish above our heads. In the forests of Crespo Island I hunted with the captain in diving suits among forests of seaweed forty feet tall, where light fell in rainbow bands from above. At Ceylon we watched pearl divers descend, and when a shark seized one poor diver in its terrible jaws, it was Captain Nemo himself who sprang forward with a dagger, and Ned Land whose unerring harpoon struck the monster dead, saving the captain's life. Nemo set the rescued man free with a bag of pearls, saying to me quietly, That Indian, sir, is an inhabitant of an oppressed country, and I am still, and shall be to my last breath, one of them.\n\nWe passed under the Isthmus of Suez through an Arabian tunnel the captain had discovered, crossing from the Red Sea to the Mediterranean in minutes. We wandered the drowned streets of Atlantis by volcanic torchlight, ten centuries of history sleeping under stone. And then came the boldest deed of all, for Captain Nemo drove the Nautilus southward through mountainous ice until, at the ninetieth degree, he stood upon black rock beneath the pole itself, unfurled his black flag bearing the golden N, and cried.\n\n\"I, Captain Nemo, take possession of this part of the globe equal to a sixth part of the known continents!\"\n\nYet the ice nearly claimed us on the return. An iceberg overthrew the Nautilus and shut her in a tunnel of solid ice, and for five days we suffocated slowly while the stored air ran out, my companions gasping, Conseil pale, Ned Land silent. Only the crew's pickaxes and the captain's genius saved us, for he melted a trench with boiling water drawn from the ship's boilers, and the last pane of ice cracked open just as we lay dying.\n\nTwo tragedies followed. Off the Bahama waters a band of monstrous poulps, devils of the deep with eight serpentine arms each, attacked the vessel, and in the frightful battle on the platform, fought with axes in the swirling ink, one sailor of the Nautilus was swept away before Ned Land's harpoon pierced the squid's heart. The captain stood weeping beside me. And soon after, a warship fired upon us, hunting the sea monster she believed us, and Captain Nemo, crying out that he was the law and the avenger, sank her utterly with the Nautilus's iron spur, then went to his room and knelt before the portrait of two young children and a woman, sobbing aloud. Who were they? What wrong had driven this genius from mankind? He never told me.\n\nAfter that Ned Land could bear no more. Escape, he begged me, tonight. I consented, though my heart was torn, for whatever the captain's crimes, I owed him the most extraordinary days of my life. We crept to the dinghy lashed to the hull off the Norwegian coast, and even unscrewed its bolts, when a dreadful word rang through the Nautilus, repeated again and again.\n\n\"The maelstrom! The maelstrom!\"\n\nThe Nautilus was caught in the famous whirlpool between the islands of Ferroe and Loffoden, the navel of the ocean, where whales themselves are dragged down and trees are worn smooth as fur. Our boat tore loose and hurled us spinning into that abyss of roaring water, and I knew no more.\n\nThus ends the voyage under the seas. How we escaped that night I cannot tell. But when I returned to consciousness I lay in a fisherman's hut on the Loffoden Isles, my two companions safe and sound beside me, and the Nautilus had vanished forever into the northern deep.\n\nShall I be believed? I do not know. It matters little after all. What I affirm is that I have crossed twenty thousand leagues under the seas, and beheld what no other living man has seen. But what has become of Captain Nemo? Did he survive the maelstrom? Will hatred be appeased in that savage heart, or did the judge disappear and the philosopher remain? To the question asked by Ecclesiastes three thousand years ago, That which is far off and exceeding deep, who can find it out, only two men now living hold the answer.\n\nCaptain Nemo, and myself.",
