    [
        'slug' => 'nature-lobo-wolf',
        'gutenberg_id' => 3031,
        'title' => 'Lobo, the King of Currumpaw',
        'author' => 'Ernest Thompson Seton',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'genres' => ['Science/Nature'],
        'quiz_difficulty' => 'easy',
        'cover' => 'covers/nature-lobo-wolf.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'A thousand-dollar bounty rides on the head of a giant gray wolf who scorns every trap and poison men devise, until hunters learn that even a king can be taken through the heart.',
        'text' => "Currumpaw is a vast cattle range in northern New Mexico, a land of rolling mesas and precious running waters. And the king whose despotic power was felt over its entire extent was an old gray wolf.\n\nOld Lobo, or the king, as the Mexicans called him, was the gigantic leader of a small band of gray wolves that had ravaged the Currumpaw valley for years. Wherever he appeared with his trusty followers, terror reigned among the cattle and wrath and despair among their owners. An ordinary wolf might howl half the night about a herdsman's camp without drawing more than a passing notice, but when the deep roar of the old king came booming down the canyon, the watcher bestirred himself and knew that in the morning he would find fresh ruin among the herds. His band numbered only five, yet each was a wolf of renown. One was a beautiful white wolf the Mexicans called Blanca, thought to be his mate. Another was a yellow wolf of remarkable swiftness who had several times run down an antelope for the pack.\n\nFor five years this band defied everything the ranchmen could devise, exacting a tribute said to reach a cow each day, always selecting the finest stock and refusing anything tainted or diseased. They were sleek and well fed, these freebooters, and fastidious. They disdained mutton too, though they sometimes amused themselves by killing it. One November night Blanca and the yellow wolf destroyed two hundred and fifty sheep apparently for the fun of it and did not eat an ounce of the flesh.\n\nA great price stood on Lobo's head at last, fully a thousand dollars, an unparalleled bounty. Poison in twenty subtle forms was put out for him, and he detected and avoided every grain. He feared only firearms, and knowing all men carried them, he never once faced a human being. His rule that the pack eat only what they themselves had killed saved them again and again, and his scent for the taint of human hands completed their immunity.\n\nA cowboy once crept up on the pack as they strove to break a herd, and saw the king lose patience with his bungling followers. Lobo left his knoll with a deep roar, charged, and the cattle scattered like the pieces of a bursting bomb. The chosen heifer had gone barely twenty-five yards when he was upon her. Seizing her by the neck he held back with all his force and threw her heels over head, turning a somersault himself, while the rest fell on and finished her in seconds. Lobo took no part in the killing. Having thrown the victim he seemed to say, Now, why could not some of you have done that at once without wasting so much time?\n\nThe cowboy poisoned the carcass in three places. Next morning the wolves had eaten the heifer but carefully cut out and thrown aside every poisoned part.\n\nHunters came against him from far and wide. Tannerey the Texan galloped up the canyon with the best of guns and horses and a pack of enormous wolf hounds. Within two miles the gray band leaped into view, and the chase grew fast and furious, until Lobo crossed a rocky canyon, scattered his pursuers, and turned at bay so that only six dogs returned that night, two of them torn terribly. On his last try Tannerey's best horse fell and died, and he rode home to Texas leaving Lobo more than ever the despot of the region. Others came with newfangled poisons and with charms, one swearing the beast was a veritable werewolf that could not be killed by ordinary means. All failed. Joe Calone even watched the king den within a thousand yards of his own house, raise a family there, and laugh at smoke and dynamite alike. There's where he lived all last summer, said Joe, pointing at the cliff face. And I couldn't do a thing with him. I was like a fool to him.\n\nThis history I heard from the cowboys in the fall of 1893 and found hard to believe until I made the acquaintance of the wily marauder myself. I had been a wolf hunter in earlier days, and when a friend who owned a Currumpaw ranch asked me to try my hand against this pack, I came gladly among the mesas. My guide would point out skeleton after skeleton with the hide still clinging. That's some of his work, he would say.\n\nI soon learned that in such rough country hounds and horses were useless, so poison and traps must do the work. A hundred devices I essayed. Once, following the hint of an old trapper, I stewed kidney fat and cheese in a china dish, cut it with a bone knife to avoid any taint of metal, hid capsules of strychnine and cyanide inside lumps of the mixture, wore gloves steeped in hot heifer blood through the whole business, and dropped baits along ten miles of trail without breathing on them. That very evening the deep bass howl rolled down the canyon. There he is, said one of the boys briefly. We'll see.\n\nAt dawn I rode his trail exulting. He had come to the first bait, sniffed, and picked it up in his mouth. Then the second, and the third. At the fourth I found my three baits piled together, and over them the king-wolf had scattered filth to express his utter contempt for my devices. After which he went about his business with the pack he guarded so well.\n\nWhen the steel traps arrived I set them out with every artifice I knew. Lobo trotted the trail in darkness and instantly detected the first, stopped the pack, scratched it carefully bare of dust until trap and chain and log lay exposed to view, and passed on, treating a dozen so that night. In a new formation I laid traps like the letter H along his path. He walked fairly between the parallel rows before he smelled danger, and then, without turning an inch to right or left, he slowly and cautiously backed on his own tracks, setting each paw exactly in its old print, till clear of the ground. Then from the side he raked clods with his hind feet until he had sprung every trap. The Angel of the wild things must have been with him.\n\nBut he might have ruled the range today but for an unfortunate alliance, for heroes unassailable when alone have often fallen through the indiscretion of a trusted ally. A cowboy put me on the truth. I saw them today, he said, and the wild one that breaks away is Blanca. Only a she-wolf would dare run ahead of the king, for were it a he-wolf, Lobo would kill him at once.\n\nSo I tried a new plan. I killed a heifer and set obvious, clumsy traps about the carcass, sure that Lobo would detect the handiwork and hold the pack away. But the head, useless offal beneath a wolf's notice, I set apart with six powerful steel traps hidden around it, my hands and boots smeared with fresh blood, coyote tracks brushed over all. Next morning the tracks told the tale. The pack had shunned the meat, but one small wolf had gone straight to the head and walked into a trap. Within a mile we found her. It was Blanca.\n\nShe galloped away dragging the fifty-pound head until its horns caught fast among the rocks, and there we closed in. She was the handsomest wolf I ever saw, her coat nearly white and in perfect condition. She turned to fight, raised her voice, and sent the long rallying cry rolling over the canyon. From far away on the mesa came a deep answer, the cry of Old Lobo. It was her last call. What followed was the inevitable tragedy, and it is one I shrank from afterward more than at the time. We threw two lassos over her neck and strained our horses apart until her limbs fell limp, and rode home exulting over the first blow struck against the pack.\n\nAll day we heard him wandering the distant mesas, searching for her. No longer the loud defiant howl, but a long plaintive wail. Blanca! Blanca! Even the stolid cowboys noticed it and said they had never heard a wolf carry on like that before. When he found the bloodstained spot his heartbroken crying was piteous beyond belief. Then he took the trail of our horses to the ranch house itself, surprised our watchdog outside, tore him to bits within fifty yards of the door, sprang one of my traps there, and tore himself loose from it. Now indeed I truly know that Blanca was his mate, I said.\n\nMy mistake was plain to me. Had I kept her alive for a decoy I might have taken him that night. Instead I gathered every trap I could command, one hundred and thirty strong steel wolf traps, set them in fours on every trail leading into the canyon, buried each log so cunningly that the eye could find no handiwork, and then trailed poor Blanca's body over every place and printed her paw marks over every trap. Next morning a cowboy met me at supper. There was a great row among the cattle in the north canyon, he said. Maybe there is something in the traps.\n\nIt was afternoon before I reached the spot, and as I drew near a great grizzly form arose from the ground. There stood Lobo, King of the Currumpaw, firm in the iron grasp of four traps. Following her trail at last, he had come recklessly down it into the snare prepared for him, and for two days and nights he had lain there, while the cattle gathered round at a safe distance to insult the fallen despot. When he saw me he rose with bristling mane and made the canyon reverberate one last time with the deep muster call of his band. None answered. Left alone in his extremity he whirled and hurled himself at me, but each trap dragged three hundred pounds, and his huge tusks ground grooves into those cruel chains that are on them to this day. Soon he sank exhausted.\n\nGrand old outlaw, hero of a thousand lawless raids, I said as I swung my lasso, in a few minutes you will be but a great load of carrion. It cannot be otherwise. But he was yet far from subdued. Before the coils fell he seized the noose and with one fierce chop cut the hard strands in two. I did not wish to spoil the royal hide with a bullet, so we threw him a stick of wood, and while his teeth held it our fresh lasso whistled through the air.\n\nThen I cried, Stay, we will not kill him. Let us take him alive to camp. With a stout stick through his mouth behind the tusks and his jaws lashed fast, he felt himself helpless and made no further resistance. He looked calmly at us and seemed to say, Well, you have got me at last. Do as you please with me. From that time he took no more notice of us.\n\nWe bore him slowly to the ranch and staked him in the pasture under collar and chain. Close up, vulgar report proved a liar. No golden collar hung on his neck, no inverted cross marked his shoulders, only a great broad scar on one haunch, the fang mark tradition assigns to Juno, leader of the Texan's wolfhounds, given the instant before he stretched her lifeless on the sand.\n\nMeat and water he refused. He lay calmly gazing with steadfast yellow eyes away past me, down the canyon gateway, over the open plains. His plains. When night fell I waited for him to call the scattered band, but he called once in extremity and none came, and he would never call again. A lion shorn of his strength, an eagle robbed of his freedom, a dove bereft of his mate, all die, it is said, of a broken heart. Who will say this grim bandit bore the threefold brunt heart whole? This only I know. When morning dawned he still lay in his position of calm repose, his body unwounded, but his spirit was gone. The old king wolf was dead.\n\nI took the chain from his neck, and a cowboy helped me carry him to the shed where lay the remains of Blanca. As we laid him beside her, the cattle man exclaimed, There, you would come to her. Now you are together again.",
